<?php

namespace App\Libraries;

/**
 * Fourteenth batch: what the assistant learned from the training videos the owner supplied (machine learning, neural networks, attention and
 * transformers, optimisers, web security basics, AI agent repos), how it reasons and checks itself, how it communicates with the owner and with
 * users, and how it handles security questions. Every answer follows the same shape: the answer first, the steps, a worked example, the reason.
 * Knowledge only: no password, key or threshold. Loaded by `php spark brain:seed`.
 */
class BrainSeed14
{
    /** @return list<array<string,mixed>> */
    public static function all(): array
    {
        $k = static fn (string $title, string $keywords, string $body, array $steps = [], array $extra = []): array => array_merge(['title' => $title, 'keywords' => $keywords, 'body' => $body, 'steps' => $steps], $extra);

        return [
            // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- machine learning (from the videos)
            $k('What is machine learning and how does a model learn?', 'what is machine learning, how does a model learn, how does ai learn, training a model, what is training, learning from data, how do machines learn, ml basics, loss gradient training',
                'A model learns by making a prediction, measuring how wrong it was, and nudging its numbers to be a little less wrong, over and over. The wrongness is the loss, the direction that reduces it is the gradient, and each small step downhill is a gradient-descent step. Repeating that over millions of examples gives a trained model; modern AI is the same idea with huge data, huge models and huge compute (data x model x compute).',
                ['Predict.', 'Compare with the real answer to get the error (the loss).', 'Ask which direction makes the loss smaller (the gradient).', 'Take a small step that way and repeat.'], ['related_hint' => 'gradient descent']),
            $k('What is gradient descent and what is the learning rate?', 'gradient descent, learning rate, what is a gradient, step size, loss function, minimise loss, why does training use small steps, optimizer basics, walk downhill',
                'Gradient descent moves every weight a small step in the direction that lowers the error: new weight = old weight - learning rate x gradient. The learning rate is the step size. Too big and the weight swings past the best value; too small and training takes forever. Example: a weight at 5 with gradient 2 and learning rate 0.1 becomes 4.8. This is also how the Brain learns each knowledge entry, with a capped step.',
                ['Compute the gradient of the error for each weight.', 'Multiply by the learning rate.', 'Subtract it from the weight.', 'Repeat until the error stops falling.']),
            $k('What is the Adam optimizer?', 'adam optimizer, what is adam, adaptive moment estimation, adaptive learning rate, adagrad, momentum optimizer, why adam, per weight step size, kingma ba',
                'Adam (adaptive moment estimation, Kingma and Ba, 2014) is gradient descent where every weight gets its own step size, worked out from two running averages: the average of recent gradients (momentum) and the average of their squares (how steep and noisy that weight is). A weight on a steep slope takes small careful steps, a weight on a flat slope takes bigger ones. It suits large problems with noisy gradients; the Brain\'s trainer uses the same idea: terms seen rarely move faster than terms trained thousands of times.',
                ['Keep a running average of the gradient (momentum).', 'Keep a running average of the squared gradient.', 'Divide the momentum by the square root of the squares to get each weight\'s own step.']),
            $k('What is dropout in a neural network?', 'dropout, what is dropout, switch off neurons, random neurons off, regularization, prevent overfitting, why dropout works, hinton dropout, neurons coin flip',
                'Dropout switches off a random part of the network at every training step so no neuron can rely on a partner being there; each one must learn something useful on its own. The survivors are scaled up so the signal stays the same size: with 4 neurons giving 2, 4, 1, 3 (total 10), switching off two leaves 2 + 3 = 5, doubled back to 10. At test time every neuron is on. Geoffrey Hinton said the idea came from bank tellers who kept changing.',
                ['Each neuron flips a coin at every step: on or off.', 'Scale the survivors up so the total stays the same.', 'At test time use all neurons.']),
            $k('What is quantization of an AI model?', 'quantization, what is quantization, 4 bit 8 bit model, compress model, reduce model size, int8 int4, shrink ai model memory, low precision weights, scaling factor rounding',
                'Quantization stores a model\'s weights as small integers (8-bit or 4-bit) instead of 32-bit floating-point numbers: divide each value by a scaling factor and round to the nearest integer; at run time scale it back up. A 100-billion-parameter model in 32-bit needs about 400 GB, far more than any GPU, but in 4-bit it is about eight times smaller. The restored weights are not exact because of the rounding, so a little accuracy is traded for a lot of memory.',
                ['Pick a scaling factor.', 'Divide and round each weight to a small integer.', 'Store the integers and the factor.', 'At inference multiply back and run as usual.']),
            $k('What is a neuron, a layer and a neural network?', 'what is a neuron, what is a neural network, what is a layer, weighted sum, bias, activation function, stack layers, deep learning basics, perceptron',
                'A neuron multiplies each input by a weight, adds the results (a weighted sum), adds a bias and squashes it through a curve. A hundred neurons side by side are a layer; stacked layers are a neural network. Example: inputs 2 and 3 with weights 0.5 and 1 and bias 1 give 2 x 0.5 + 3 x 1 + 1 = 5 before the curve. Stack enough layers and the network can bend space until a straight line can separate the classes.',
                ['Multiply inputs by weights and add them.', 'Add the bias.', 'Pass the sum through an activation curve.', 'Put many neurons in a layer and stack layers.']),
            $k('What is attention in a transformer (query, key and value)?', 'what is attention, self attention, query key value, how does attention work, transformer attention, qkv, attention scores softmax, multi head attention, why divide by square root, causal masking',
                'Attention lets every word look at every other word and decide how relevant each one is. Each word becomes a query (the question it asks), a key (what it offers as an answer) and a value (the content it hands over). Query-times-key gives similarity scores; they are divided by the square root of the key size so the numbers stay stable, turned into percentages with softmax, and used to mix the values. Multi-head attention runs several of these at once so the model captures different relationships. Causal masking hides future words during training so the model cannot cheat when predicting the next word.',
                ['Turn each word into a query, key and value.', 'Score query against every key and divide by the square root of the key size.', 'Softmax the scores.', 'Mix the values with those weights.', 'Hide future words (causal mask) for next-word prediction.']),
            $k('What is a transformer and how does ChatGPT work?', 'what is a transformer, how does chatgpt work, how do llms work, large language model, next word prediction, transformer block, gpt explained, how does gpt predict the next word',
                'A transformer block combines attention (every word scores how relevant every other word is) with a small neural network. Stack dozens of such blocks and train them to predict the next word of text from the internet, and you get a large language model such as ChatGPT. It writes one word at a time, so each word must be chosen carefully: it produces a probability for every possible next word and picks from the likely ones.',
                ['Split text into tokens and turn them into vectors.', 'Pass them through stacked attention + network blocks.', 'Get probabilities for the next token.', 'Choose one, append it and repeat.']),
            $k('How does a vision transformer read an image (patches and embeddings)?', 'vision transformer, vit, patch embedding, position embedding, cls token, how does gpt read an image, image patches, how transformer sees pictures, 196 patches',
                'A vision transformer cuts the picture into squares called patches (the original uses 196 patches), flattens each patch (4 by 4 pixels x 3 colours = 48 numbers) and a learned matrix turns it into a vector: the patch embedding, like a word embedding. Because attention ignores order, every grid position also gets its own learned position embedding that is added in. An extra CLS token, a learned blank vector, is put in front; after 12 layers (ViT base) only the CLS vector is kept and one last layer turns it into a score for each class.',
                ['Cut the image into patches.', 'Turn each patch into a vector (patch embedding).', 'Add a position embedding to each.', 'Add the CLS token and run the transformer layers.', 'Read the class scores from the CLS vector.']),
            $k('What is a convolutional neural network (CNN)?', 'convolutional neural network, cnn, what is convolution, filter feature map, image recognition network, kernel slide over image, edges textures shapes, convolution layer',
                'A convolution slides a small filter across an image to detect one local pattern. Different learned filters detect different patterns and give feature maps; stacked feature maps make a convolutional layer, and stacked layers learn edges first, then textures, shapes and finally whole objects. Combining those features to say what is in the picture is a convolutional neural network.',
                ['Slide a small filter over the pixels.', 'Collect the responses as a feature map.', 'Stack filters and layers: edges, textures, shapes, objects.', 'Combine the features to classify the image.']),
            $k('What are embeddings and self-supervised learning (JEPA)?', 'what is an embedding, embeddings explained, self supervised learning, jepa, joint embedding predictive architecture, predict hidden part, masked prediction, learn without labels',
                'An embedding is a list of numbers that places something (a word, a patch, an image) in a space where similar things are close. Self-supervised learning hides part of the data, asks the model to predict the hidden part\'s embedding, compares the prediction with the real embedding and uses the error to improve; no human labels are needed. JEPA (joint-embedding predictive architecture) connects the predictor, the embeddings and that learning loop.',
                ['Turn the data into embeddings.', 'Hide part of it and predict that part.', 'Compare prediction and reality: the error.', 'Use the error to improve the model.']),
            $k('How do k-means clustering and PCA work?', 'k-means clustering, kmeans, what is pca, principal component analysis, eigenvector eigenvalue, covariance matrix, unsupervised learning, cluster centers, dimensionality reduction, how to group data without labels',
                'k-means needs no labels: pick k centres, assign each point to its nearest centre, move each centre to the average of its points, and repeat until the centres stop moving. PCA finds the directions along which the data varies most: they are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix (an eigenvector keeps its direction when the matrix transforms it; its eigenvalue says how much it is stretched). Projecting the data onto the top directions flattens the rest and keeps most of the information.',
                ['k-means: choose centres, assign points, average, repeat until stable.', 'PCA: build the covariance matrix, take its eigenvectors, project the data on the biggest ones.']),
            $k('What are the main machine learning algorithms in one minute?', 'machine learning algorithms, linear regression, logistic regression, k nearest neighbors, naive bayes, support vector machine, decision tree, random forest, gradient boosting, ml models list, which model should i use',
                'Linear regression draws the line with the smallest total error; logistic regression bends it into an S to give a probability; k-nearest neighbours takes a vote of the closest points; naive Bayes scores each feature alone and multiplies; a support vector machine finds the widest street between two classes; a decision tree cuts the data with yes/no questions; a random forest lets a hundred different trees vote; gradient boosting stacks small trees that each fix what is still wrong; k-means groups points without labels; PCA flattens the data along its strongest directions; a neural network bends space until a line can split it; a transformer lets every token look at every other; diffusion learns to remove noise to make pictures. Which one wins depends on the data, so test them on real data.',
                []),
            // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- security basics (from the videos)
            $k('How does a JWT (JSON Web Token) work?', 'what is jwt, json web token, how does jwt work, jwt signature, header payload signature, hs256, can someone fake a jwt, token secret, why jwt is safe, 401 token',
                'A JWT has three parts: header (the algorithm, e.g. HS256), payload (the claims such as user id and role) and signature. The server signs header + payload with a SECRET that never leaves the server and puts the signature in the token. Anyone can read the payload, but nobody can change it without the secret: if someone edits role "user" to "admin", the signature no longer matches, the server signs the payload again, gets a different signature and answers 401. So a JWT is readable by everyone and forgeable by no one; never put secrets inside it.',
                ['Server signs header + payload with its SECRET.', 'The token is sent to the user.', 'On each request the server recomputes the signature.', 'Same signature: 200 OK. Different signature: 401.']),
            $k('What is an API gateway?', 'api gateway, what is an api gateway, one door for every request, rate limit 429, auth 401, routing to services, gateway authentication rate limiting, why use a gateway',
                'An API gateway is one door in front of all the services of a system. It does three jobs in one place: authentication (no valid token gets 401), rate limiting (a client that sends too many requests gets 429) and routing (the path picks the service, for example /products or /orders). The services behind it sit on a private network and never see the blocked bots.',
                ['Request arrives at the gateway.', 'Check the token (401 if missing or wrong).', 'Check the rate limit (429 if spamming).', 'Route by path to the right service.']),
            $k('What are the common web attacks and how do I recognise them?', 'common web attacks, sql injection, xss cross site scripting, path traversal, brute force password guessing, directory scanning, what does an attack look like in the log, how to recognise an attack, security basics, attack signs',
                'In a web server log the usual signs are: SQL injection (words like UNION SELECT, OR 1=1, SLEEP( or information_schema in the address), script injection or XSS (<script>, javascript:, onerror=), path traversal (../ or /etc/passwd), probes for secret files (/.env, /.git, backups, wp-login, phpMyAdmin) answered with 403/404, scanner tools in the user-agent (sqlmap, nikto, nmap), a burst of failed requests from one address, and many failed sign-ins for different names (password guessing). The defence is the same each time: prepared statements, escaped output, no secret files in the web root, rate limits and blocked addresses. I can check the logs for these signs when you ask me about attacks.',
                ['Ask me "any attack in the last hour?" for a live report.', 'Look at the address: inside the lab network or outside.', 'Decide: ignore a known device, or block the address.']),
            // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- AI agent repos (from the videos)
            $k('What are AI agent repos like agency-agents, Claude Red and money printer?', 'agency agents github, 232 ai agents, claude red security skills, offensive security skills for claude, money printer turbo, ui ux pro max skill, design studio repos, github ai agent repos, claude skills repos',
                'From the videos: "agency-agents" is a GitHub repo of 232 specialised AI agents in 16 departments (engineering, design, marketing, sales, finance ...); "Claude Red" is a free collection of offensive-security skills (for example SQL-injection tests) that is meant for testing your own systems; "ui-ux-pro-max-skill" gives an agent 88 styles and 192 palettes to pick from before it builds a screen; "money printer" makes a short video from a topic (script, voice, captions, music) and the idea is still yours. Treat such repos as ideas and read the code and licence before installing anything.',
                []),
            // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- reasoning and self-checking
            $k('How do you reason about a problem?', 'how do you reason, how do you think, how do you solve problems, logical reasoning, think step by step, how do you analyse a request, your thinking process, how do you decide, reasoning steps, problem solving method',
                'I reason in five steps: 1) understand what is asked and what is needed behind it (the intent), 2) list the facts I have (the Lab Reference, the page, the status, the logs), 3) match them with what I know and check each step against the data instead of guessing, 4) answer with the result first, then the steps and the reason, 5) say how sure I am and what would change my mind. If a fact is missing I ask for it; if two answers fit I tell you both.',
                ['Understand the request and the need behind it.', 'List the facts.', 'Check each step against the data.', 'Answer: result, steps, reason.', 'State the confidence and what is uncertain.']),
            $k('How do you check your own answer before replying?', 'how do you check your answer, do you verify, double check, are you guessing, how do you avoid mistakes, self check, verify before answering, how do you know you are right, error checking',
                'Before I reply I check: does the answer match every word of the question, is the top match clearly better than the runner-up (otherwise I say it is ambiguous), does the data I quote come from the live system, and is my confidence honest (one-word questions are never shown as certain). If the evidence is weak I say so instead of sounding sure, and I log the question so my knowledge can be improved.',
                []),
            // ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- talking with the owner and users
            $k('How do you talk to the owner and to users?', 'how do you talk to me, how do you communicate, who is gie, my name is gie, talk to me like a person, how do you speak, what should i call you, communicate clearly, speak to me, tone of voice, owner of the system',
                'The owner of the system is Gie (Synology ID 426) and I call him Gie; every other user is called by their first name. I listen for what you need, not only for the words: a question, a how-to, a status check, a task, a security check, or a complaint. I answer like a person would: one short human opening when it helps ("Understood, Gie, I treat this as urgent"), then the answer first, the steps, an example and the reason, in plain words. I can show the answer as text, read it aloud, or both, and I take your voice by microphone.',
                ['Tell me what you need, in your own words.', 'I answer with the result first and offer the steps.', 'Say "simpler", "why?" or "step by step" and I change how I explain.', 'Press the speaker button to hear me, the microphone to talk to me.']),
            $k('Can you talk to me and listen to my voice?', 'can you talk, can you speak, voice reply, read the answer aloud, talk to me, speak to me, can you hear me, voice chat, microphone, speaker button, text to speech, speech to text, hands free',
                'Yes. In the chat there is a speaker button: when it is on I read every answer aloud while the text stays on the screen, so you can listen or read. The microphone button lets you speak your request and sends it when you stop talking. Speaking stops as soon as you press the microphone or start typing, so you can interrupt me. Voice depends on your browser (Chrome or Edge work best) and asks permission to use the microphone.',
                ['Press the speaker button to turn voice replies on or off.', 'Press the microphone, say your request, and wait for the answer.', 'Press the speaker again, or type, to make me stop.']),
            $k('Someone is attacking my network: what do you do?', 'someone is attacking my network, we are being attacked, network under attack, hacker in the system, monitor my network, watch for attacks, incoming attack, respond to attack, intrusion detected, report attacks to me, security monitoring',
                'I check the web server log and the sign-in log for attack patterns (SQL or script injection, path traversal, secret-file probes, scanner tools, bursts of failed requests, password guessing), tell you what I found with the address, how often, whether it came from inside or outside the lab, and what I recommend. I do not block anything myself: blocking an address changes the web server settings, so I prepare the exact rule and you approve it. I see web requests and sign-ins, not port scans or traffic that never reaches the web server; for that the Windows firewall log or a network monitor is needed.',
                ['Ask "any attack in the last hour?" (or today).', 'I report: what, from which address, how many times, how serious.', 'You decide: ignore, block, lock an account.', 'Tell me which address to block and I prepare the rule for you to approve.']),
        ];
    }
}
